==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|abacter}}

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|LL.|abactor||cattle rustler}}, from {{m|la|abigō||drive away}}; from {{m|la|ab||from, away from}} + {{m|la|agō||drive}}.<ref>{{R:MW3 1976|page=3}}</ref>

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈæˌbæk.tɚ/|/æˈbæk.tɚ/|a=GenAm}}
* {{audio|en|EN-abactor.ogg}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|legal|archaic}} One who [[steal]]s and drives away [[cattle]] or beasts by [[herd]]s or [[drove]]s; a cattle [[rustler]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:rustler}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=A Paraphrase and Annotations Upon the Books of the Psalms|author=H. Hammond|passage={{...}} not only from straying, but, as in time of warr, from invaders and '''abactors''' {{...}}|year=1659}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1992|author=Okkūr Mācāttiyar|chapter_tlr=K.G. Seshadri|title=Purananuru 279|journal=Indian Literature|volume=35|number=149|page=27|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.140079/page/n406/mode/1up|text=But yesterday, / it was her husband / Who’d lost his life in the fight / As he beat the '''abactors''' back, / Who tried to seize their cattle.}}

====Hyponyms====
* {{l|en|horse thief}}, {{l|en|sheepstealer}}, {{l|en|napper}} {{qualifier|obsolete}}

