==Latin==
{{was fwotd|la|2022|August|9}}

===Etymology===
From {{m|la|abigō||drive away}}, from {{m|la|ab||from, away from}} + {{m|la|agō||drive}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|abāctor}}

===Noun===
{{la-noun|abāctor<3>}}

# A [[cattle]] [[thief]]; {{l|en|abactor}} or [[rustler]].
#* {{Q|la|Apuleius|Metamorphoses|7|26|quote=...meum vero Bellerophontem '''abactorem''' indubitatum cruentumque percussorem criminantes...|trans=...and as for my Bellerophon, they accused him of being an undoubted '''rustler''' and a bloody murderer.}}
#* {{Q|la|Paul.|Sent.|V|18|quote='''Abactores''' sunt qui unum equum, duas equas, totidemque boves, vel capram decem, aut porcos quinque abegerint.|trans='''Rustlers''' are those who drive away one stallion, two mares, as many cattle, or ten goats, or five pigs.}}
#* {{Q|la|Isid.|Orig.|10|14|quote='''Abactor''' est fur iumentorum, et pecorum, quem vulgo abigeum vocant, ab abigendo scilicet.|trans='''Abactor''' is a thief of draft animals and domestic animals, whom they call in vulgar Latin ''abigeus'', naturally derived from ''abigendo''.}}
# A man who [[abduct]]s.
#* {{Q|la|Firm.|Astr.|Liber VI|31|6|quote=Si vero in aquosis signis fuerint constituti, pecorum '''abactores''' efficient, insequentibus hominibus minaci semper gladio resistentes.|trans=But if they are arranged in the water signs, they create '''abductors''' of domestic animals, opposing chasing men with an ever-threatening sword.}}
#* {{Q|la|Firm.|De Err.|Cap V|quote=Virum vero '''abactorem''' bovum colentes sacra eius ad ignis transferunt potestatem, sicut propheta eius tradidit nobis dicens...|trans=Their sacrifices, worshipping that man, '''abductor''' of the bull (Mithras), bring power to the fires, as their prophet imparted to us, saying...}}

